---
/*
  The persistent chrome: the signature and MENU together, top left, and nothing
  else. Both at label scale.

  Revised 2026-08-02 (owner's call). MENU moved from the top right to sit beside
  the signature. This is not tidying, it removes a defect found in verification:
  the two marks used to sit at opposite ends of the same band of photograph, and
  a photograph can be dark at one end and bright at the other, so one polarity
  value could not keep both legible. Sharing a single local background means one
  value is always right for both (DESIGN-PLAN section 14).

  Revised 2026-08-01 (owner's call). It is fixed, so the mark stays visible over
  full-bleed media at all times, which is exactly what the primary reference
  does: Sorcinelli keeps its hairline signature in the top left over every
  full-screen image. A brand hiding its own name on every page except home is
  not restraint.

  IT FLOATS AGAIN, OVER A PHOTOGRAPH THAT BEGINS AT PIXEL ZERO (2026-08-12,
  section 115). THE WHITE BAND WAS CORRECT FOR THE EVIDENCE AT THE TIME AND IS
  SUPERSEDED.

  WHAT THE BAND WAS FOR, because none of it was wrong. It used to sit ON the
  photography and take a polarity measured per image (`overlayChrome`), swapped
  by an observer as frames passed beneath. Measured across the site, that
  arrangement put the brand's own name under 4.5:1 on 118 of 152 audit faults,
  and 71 of 97 photograph placements had NO legible side. So the band took its
  own height in the flow, in page ground, and the mark became ink on paper at
  19.6:1 everywhere (section 87, the owner's call, against rendered
  alternatives).

  WHAT CHANGED IS THE EVIDENCE, NOT THE ARITHMETIC. All 77 reference
  screenshots have now been opened rather than summarised (section 107,
  `docs/SURVEY-RIFERIMENTI.md`). Both reference sites begin at pixel zero and
  float their chrome, and Rick Owens solves legibility by RE-COLOURING THE MARKS
  AGAINST WHAT THEY LAND ON — white on the dark hero, dark blue on the yellow
  band, white again on the mountain. He does not veil, plate or strap anything.

  AND THE OLD MEASUREMENT WAS OF A DIFFERENT OBJECT. 71 of 97 counted a band
  SPANNING THE FULL WIDTH, which necessarily holds both extremes of a photograph
  at once — that is why no single value could fix it. This chrome is one CLUSTER
  at the left, the signature and MENU sharing a few hundred pixels of picture
  (the section 14 move that put them together). A local region has a legible
  side far more often than a 1440px strap does, which is the whole reason the
  reference can do this and the old band could not.

  SO: the negative margin is back, the band paints nothing, and the marks take
  the FRAME'S OWN MEASURED `overlay` — the value the owner already sets per
  image in the studio. Nothing new is measured and no observer runs: the page
  knows which photograph it opens on and says so once, at build time.

  WHERE THERE IS NO PHOTOGRAPH AT THE TOP, THE BAND STAYS. /process and
  /contact open on a text surface, so `over` is null there and the chrome takes
  its height in paper exactly as it has since section 87. This is not a fallback
  that might be forgotten — it is the default, and floating is the thing a page
  has to ask for.

  THE NO-SCRIM RULE SURVIVES INTACT (standing rule 11). Nothing is laid over the
  photograph, nothing is faded, and no value between ink and paper is painted
  anywhere. A later session must not "restore" a veil under the marks believing
  it is putting something back — there was never a veil, and the alternative
  that used one was rendered, measured and rejected (section 86). The answer to
  a mark that will not sit on a photograph is the OWNER'S `overlay` value, or a
  different photograph. It is never a grey.
*/
import Signature from "./Signature.astro";
import Menu from "./Menu.astro";
import {localePath, type Locale} from "../lib/locales";
import {overlayTheme, type OverlayPolarity} from "../lib/media";
import {t} from "../i18n/ui";

interface Props {
  /**
   * The polarity of the PAGE. The menu panel inverts against it, so opening the
   * menu performs the site's own two-state idea (DESIGN-PLAN section 61).
   */
  pageTheme?: "light" | "dark";
  /**
   * The measured `overlay` of the photograph this page OPENS on, when it opens
   * on one (section 115). Present: the chrome floats on that photograph and the
   * marks take its polarity. Absent: the chrome is a paper band in the flow,
   * which is what every text-first page gets and is the default.
   */
  over?: OverlayPolarity | null;
  locale: Locale;
}

const {locale, pageTheme = "light", over = null} = Astro.props;

/*
  THE MARKS' POLARITY IS THE FRAME'S, NOT THE PAGE'S. `overlayTheme` is the one
  place the mapping lives and reading it backwards makes the mark invisible, so
  it is called and never reimplemented (lib/media.ts).

  While floating, the header's own `--fg` must come from this value and NOT from
  the page, which is why `data-theme` goes on the element rather than a class:
  the menu summary, the signature fill and the focus ring all resolve through
  the same token, so one attribute moves all three together.
*/
const floating = over !== null;
const markTheme = floating ? overlayTheme(over) : undefined;
---

{/*
  STICKY EITHER WAY, and that is unchanged (section 70): it pins to the top so
  the marks are always reachable, and while a page still has a shipping line it
  starts below it and can never collide with it.

  WHAT `chrome-float` DOES, and it is two properties: the negative margin gives
  the band zero height in the flow so the photograph begins at pixel zero
  underneath it, and the background stops painting. Nothing else. There is no
  gradient, no plate and no blur — see the note above.
*/}
<header
  id="site-chrome"
  data-theme={markTheme}
  class:list={[
    "text-fg sticky inset-x-0 top-0 z-30 flex h-chrome items-center gap-u3 px-margin",
    floating ? "chrome-float" : "bg-bg",
  ]}
>
  <a href={localePath(locale)} aria-label={t(locale, "home")} class="block">
    <Signature class="h-sig-corner" decorative />
  </a>
  <Menu locale={locale} theme={pageTheme} />

</header>
